 - The guide's [global scope](../guide/interop/global-scope.md#a-global-some-hosts-lack).
 - Mutations: the `typeof` unwritten, of any path, of the crate's own
   function.
+
+## A binding coerced to a function pointer (#1785)
+
+A valid fn-pointer coercion/cast used to instantiate anonymous impl Trait
+parameters does not change which global is named. One origin rule now follows
+the unique MIR initializer and copies to the reified function item, for both a
+single feature-test argument and each tuple-path step. Unknown or reassigned
+pointers are not guessed. The existing binding/global/Math rules then apply;
+ordinary Rust functions remain refused, including behind a cast.
+
+is_function uses the same origin before deciding whether an argument is a
+callback value. A known namespaced binding must retain all missing-root guards,
+not read the namespace first. This is case A of ADR 0262: rustc checks the
+coercion and the same global is asked without evaluating or calling it.
+There is no native host-global counterpart; regression tests vary actual JS
+globals and getters and prove one read and zero calls. Mutations remove origin
+recovery, confuse binding/value tests, and pick an ambiguous initializer.
